  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Virginia has received $67.8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. That total comes from 286,137 awards with an average award size of $237K, and obligations fell 43.7% year over year. Looking back by fiscal year, obligations were $77.9B in FY21, $89.4B in FY22, $106B in FY23, $110B in FY24, and $121B in FY25, before the current-year partial total of $67.8B for FY26. The current picture reflects large, concentrated buys and many smaller awards given the average award size. This snapshot is fiscal year to date and therefore partial for the current year, so the FY26 totals are not full-year figures.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive Virginia federal spending?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense accounts for $27.5B of obligations in FY2026 to date, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $17.3B. Other major buyers include the Department of Homeland Security with $5.3B and the General Services Administration with $4.5B. Several civilian and civil agencies also appear, including HHS at $2.9B and DOJ at $2B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which contractors and industries dominate the state?",
        "body": "Top contractors in FY2026 to date include TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP at $7.7B and OPTUM PUBLIC SECTOR SOLUTIONS, INC. at $6.6B, with HUNTINGTON INGALLS INC and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION each at $2.7B. Leading industries are Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $14.4B and Computer Systems Design Services at $7.9B, followed by Other Computer Related Services at $5.1B and Engineering Services at $4.7B."
      }
    ]
  },
